  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nRadar and GOES imagery this afternoon shows developing showers\nand thunderstorms across the region with storms becoming more\nwidespread in response to the approaching cold front and\ndiurnally driven instability. Temperatures have managed to warm\ninto the lower 80s in many locations with dew points well into\nthe 60s south of the mountains. The latest SPC mesoanalysis\nshows up to around 1000 J/KG of MLCAPE stretched across portions\nof interior western ME along with 700-500mb lapse rates of ~6\nC/Km. Effective bulk shear is modest at around 25 kts, which may\nhelp to sustain some stronger updrafts through early this\nevening, mainly across interior western ME. Any storms will be\ncapable of producing locally strong to damaging winds along with\nperhaps hail. Showers and storms will wane this evening behind\nthe front.\n\nA much drier airmass will move in behind the front tonight with\nlow temperatures falling into the lower to middle 40s across\nsome interior valleys with 50s elsewhere. High pressure will\nbuild across New England this weekend, allowing for tranquil\nweather conditions with warm afternoons and cool nights. Highs\non Saturday will be into the upper 70s to near 80 south of the\nmountain with highs on Sunday around 5 degrees warmer. Lows each\nnight will be into the 40s and 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper trough swinging through the Canadian Maritimes will send a \ncold front through northern New England Sunday night. This front \nwill bring chances for showers and thunderstorms, mainly from the \nmountains to central Maine and points northward late Sunday. Latest \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance favor mostly dry conditions \nMonday as high pressure builds in from the north and the cold\nfront settles over southern New England as a stationary front.\n\nA compact upper low over Canadian Prairies Tuesday will slowly\ntranslate east through the second half of next week. This\nfeature will attempt to shift the stationary front northward as\na warm front late Tuesday. Models diverge on how far north rain\nchances get on Tuesday with some solutions keeping all but far\nsouthern NH mostly dry. The latest NBM expands chance PoPs\n(30-50%) northeast across the area through Tuesday afternoon\nwhich seems reasonable given the spread in solutions. Model\nspread also exists with respect to the eastward progression of\nthe upper low and a potential leading short wave crossing\nWednesday and Thursday. This has resulted in a broad brush of 50\nto 60 percent PoPs Wednesday through Thursday while it is likely\nthat it will not be raining continuously through this time.\nOverall, the pattern supports some beneficial rainfall from the\nmiddle to end of the work week.",
